-- Passage rg6-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('715d8257-deb1-5d23-8b8b-a0474ce58853', 'rg6-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Heron Kite$q$, $q$Amara Osei had spent three weekends building her kite with her grandfather. It was shaped like a blue heron, with long paper legs that trailed behind it in the wind. The judging at the Harbor Park kite festival would begin at noon, and the judges only scored kites that were in the air.

At eleven thirty, the heron rose on the first try. Amara let out the line slowly, the way her grandfather had shown her, until the bird hung high above the crowd. He gave her a thumbs-up from his folding chair.

Then she heard crying behind her. A small boy in a striped hat was holding a stick wrapped in a hopeless knot of string. His red kite lay face down in the grass, its tail tied around its own frame.

Amara looked up at her heron. She looked at the boy. Then she wound her line in hand over hand, set the heron gently on the grass, and knelt beside him.

It took twenty minutes to work the knot loose. By the time the red kite climbed into the sky, the judges were already walking back to their table with their clipboards.

Amara's grandfather did not say anything about the contest. On the walk to the car, he carried the heron himself, holding it carefully in front of him as if it had won first prize. That night, he cut a small red ribbon from a spool in his toolbox drawer and tied it to the heron's leg.$q$, 'draft')
