-- Passage rg6-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('272207d6-2d37-57ce-8419-87f1a4cfa486', 'rg6-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Message for Millbrook$q$, $q$In the summer of 1889, eleven-year-old Lucia Ferraro swept the floor of her uncle's telegraph office every morning in the town of Millbrook. While she swept, she listened. The brass sounder on the counter clicked out messages from towns all along the railroad, and Uncle Paolo wrote each one down on a yellow form.

At night, Lucia tapped out short and long beats on her bedroom windowsill with a pencil. She had copied the code onto a card that she kept in her apron pocket. When she asked her uncle to teach her properly, he laughed and said the key was not a toy.

One hot afternoon in August, Uncle Paolo stepped out to buy ice. Five minutes later, the sounder began to click. Lucia froze with the broom in her hands. The message was for Millbrook, and no one else was there to take it.

She grabbed a yellow form and a pencil. Her hand shook at first, but the clicks were slow and steady, and she knew every letter. CIRCUS TRAIN DELAYED BY FLOOD AT RIVER JUNCTION. ARRIVES TOMORROW NOON. TELL MAYOR.

When Uncle Paolo came back, Lucia held out the form without a word. He read it twice. Then he tapped a short reply to the station at River Junction, asking them to repeat the message. He listened, comparing each word to her page.

That evening, the mayor posted a notice moving the circus parade to the next day. The following morning, Lucia found a second stool beside her uncle's at the counter. On the counter in front of it lay a stack of blank yellow forms.$q$, 'draft')
