-- Passage rg6-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1e25dd22-8ff9-57fc-911d-7a5001c79910', 'rg6-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Winter Count$q$, $q$Every December, Nikhil Banerjee's grandfather volunteered for the winter bird count at Cedar Marsh. This year, for the first time, Nikhil was allowed to come along. He had studied a field guide for weeks, and he hoped to spot a snowy owl, which had been reported twice nearby.

They arrived before sunrise. Grandfather handed him a clipboard with a tally sheet and a pencil tied to it with string. "Your job is to count," he said. "Every bird, not just the exciting ones."

By nine o'clock, Nikhil had marked forty-two house sparrows, nineteen starlings, and more mallard ducks than he could keep track of. His fingers were stiff with cold. Twice he scanned the fence posts for a white shape, and twice he found only a plastic bag caught on the wire.

Near the end of the boardwalk, a small brown bird hopped out from under the reeds. Nikhil almost skipped it. It looked like the sparrows he had been counting all morning. Then he noticed a thin yellow stripe above its eye.

He checked the field guide. Then he checked it again. He tugged his grandfather's sleeve and pointed.

Grandfather raised his binoculars and was quiet for a long moment. Then he took out his phone and called the count leader. "Marisol," he said, "you will want to come to the boardwalk. Bring the camera."

On the drive home, Nikhil realized he had not thought about the snowy owl in over an hour. The tally sheet sat on his lap, and next to one line he had drawn a small star.$q$, 'draft')
